---
import TrainingLayout from "../../layouts/TrainingLayout.astro";
import Snippet from "../../components/training/Snippet.astro";
import LiveDemo from "../../components/training/LiveDemo.astro";
import ApiLink from "../../components/training/ApiLink.astro";
import ChapterLink from "../../components/training/ChapterLink.astro";
import Callout from "../../components/training/Callout.astro";
import VirtualScroll from "../../components/training/diagrams/VirtualScroll.astro";
---
<TrainingLayout slug="virtual-scrolling">

  <h2 id="idea">The idea</h2>
  <p>
    A screen shows maybe 20 rows at a time. So why build 100,000? <strong>Virtual scrolling</strong> keeps the data in
    memory and puts only the visible rows into the DOM. When the user scrolls, the same few rows are filled with
    other data.
  </p>
  <VirtualScroll />
  <ol>
    <li>An empty <strong>spacer</strong> is as high as <em>all</em> rows together – the browser draws a real scrollbar for it.</li>
    <li>On every scroll event: first visible row = <code>scrollTop / rowHeight</code>, number of rows = viewport height / row height.</li>
    <li>Only these rows go into the DOM, moved down by <code>first × rowHeight</code>.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2 id="code">Built by hand in 20 lines</h2>
  <Snippet file="demos/virtual-scroll.ts" region="dom" lang="html" title="the DOM" />
  <Snippet file="demos/virtual-scroll.ts" region="scroller" title="virtual-scroll.ts" />

  <h2 id="try">Try it</h2>
  <LiveDemo name="virtual-scroll" title="Virtual scrolling by hand" height={380}
            caption="Switch to 1,000,000 rows: the number of DOM elements stays the same, rendering takes about a millisecond." />
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th class="num">Rows</th><th class="num">DOM elements</th><th class="num">Load</th><th class="num">Render per scroll step</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td class="num">100,000</td><td class="num">≈ 360</td><td class="num">0.2 s</td><td class="num">≈ 1 ms</td></tr>
      <tr><td class="num">1,000,000</td><td class="num">≈ 360</td><td class="num">1.1 s</td><td class="num">≈ 1 ms</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>Compare that with the 1.2 million DOM elements of <ChapterLink slug="why-big-tables-freeze" label="the naive table" />.</p>

  <h2 id="limits">Why a library then?</h2>
  <p>The 20 lines only scroll vertically. Everything else from chapter 1 is still missing – plus a few hard problems:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Horizontal virtual scrolling</strong> – 40 or 400 columns should not all be in the DOM either.</li>
    <li><strong>Fixed header and fixed columns</strong> that stay in place while the rest scrolls.</li>
    <li><strong>Different row heights</strong> – then <code>scrollTop / rowHeight</code> no longer works.</li>
    <li><strong>Browser limits</strong>: 1 million rows × 30 px = 30 million pixels. Chrome allows about 33 million, Firefox
      much less. A spacer for 2 million rows simply stops working.</li>
    <li>Sorting, filtering, selection, keyboard, copy, export, renderers, editing …</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The GuiExpert Table virtualises rows <strong>and</strong> columns, caps the scroll height so that 2 million rows run
    smoothly, and – if all rows have the same height – finds the first visible row in O(1) via
    <ApiLink name="AreaModelIf.getConstantRowHeight" /> (<ChapterLink slug="custom-area-model" />).
    At runtime <ApiLink name="TableApi.getFirstVisibleRowIndex" /> tells you which row is at the top.
  </p>

  <Callout type="tip" title="See it with two million rows">
    <p>The showcases <a href="/showcase/large/run/">Large dataset</a> and <a href="/showcase/fps/run/">Render speed</a>
      run the real table with 2 million rows and show the frame rate while you scroll.</p>
  </Callout>

  <h2 id="next">Next</h2>
  <p>Build it yourself or use a library – and if a library, which one? <ChapterLink slug="build-buy-or-guiexpert" /> compares the options honestly.</p>
</TrainingLayout>
